{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Fill… and my goblet…</b> One needs to know the reason for Joseph's [involvement] in these matters. And if it was to distress them — surely his actions prove that he had already concluded peace in his heart, and he ate and drank with them and showed them peace. Perhaps, however, he intended three things: the first, so that their sin of the theft — that they stole him — would be atoned for them; he would bring matters of suspicion upon them so that the shame would fall upon the very species of the sin itself relating to Joseph, and their sin would be atoned. The second, to see whether they would risk their lives for Benjamin, and through this means he would recognize that they measure a measure of brotherly love toward their brother, and he would atone for their sin. The third, to hint to them the matter of the theft — perhaps they would begin to sense that there is someone who is aware of their deeds in this house. And so we find that he hinted the matter to them in other details as well, as it is written (Genesis 43:33): ״וַיֵּשְׁבוּ לְפָנָיו הַבְּכוֹר כִּבְכוֹרָתוֹ״ etc., and such instances are many."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:48:28.779302205Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}